<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # How Can We Help the Environment? – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> An Environmental Project</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Can We Help the Environment?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Write short scripts for plays collaboratively about familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can draft short scripts about everyday recycling activities (e.g., 'We wash the plastic and glass bottles every day.').<br>Can write descriptive texts on recycling projects (e.g., 'We collect the plastic bottles and sort them by color.').<br>Can collaborate with peers to write and perform short scripts about environmental projects (e.g., 'Let's write a play about how we recycle at school. I'll write the first part, and you can add the next part.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage activates prior knowledge and sets the context for the lesson through engaging activities, teacher modeling of language, and clarification of new concepts or vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different environmental actions (e.g., planting a tree, recycling bottles, cleaning a park). Students individually write one or two sentences in their notebooks describing what they see in each picture using familiar vocabulary like 'clean', 'plant', 'recycle'. They can also list key words associated with each action.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes an example sentence on the board about one picture: "We will plant trees in the garden next week." The teacher models the pronunciation of 'will' /wɪl/ and its contracted form 'I'll' /aɪl/ by saying "I'll plant a tree too." The teacher also models segmenting a multisyllabic word, "pre-sen-ta-tion," as a quick pronunciation check for a word they might use later when talking about projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we doing this action now, or in the future?" (Expected answer: In the future.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'plant' a noun or a verb in 'We will plant trees'?" (Expected answer: A verb.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">New language or content is introduced within an authentic context, connected to the lesson's scenario. Students engage with the material to grasp its meaning and purpose.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> "An Environmental Project". The teacher presents a short script for a simple play about a school recycling project. The script is short, 3-4 lines per character, and clearly uses present simple, future simple, and imperatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short script (genre: play script; register: informal; format: dialogue with character names and actions). Example:
                <br><b>Scene:</b> The Classroom
                <br><b>Characters:</b> MARIO, ANA, TEACHER
                <br><br><b>MARIO:</b> Hey Ana, what will we do with these plastic bottles?
                <br><b>ANA:</b> We will clean them first. We wash the plastic and glass bottles every day.
                <br><b>MARIO:</b> Oh, right! Then what?
                <br><b>TEACHER:</b> Put paper in the blue bin, and bottles in the green bin. Let's start our recycling project!</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Linguistic Competence - Use of basic grammatical structures (present simple, future simple, imperatives) and vocabulary related to environmental actions to understand and produce simple written texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the script individually.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist question: "What is the play about?" (Expected answer: A recycling project / How to recycle.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "They will throw away the bottles." (False) "Ana washes the bottles every day." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Matching: Match the character to their action (e.g., Mario - asks about bottles, Teacher - gives instructions).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8-10 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students practice the target language with a focus on accuracy, gradually moving from controlled to more semi-controlled activities. The emphasis is on correct form and structure.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences and short paragraphs using future simple (for plans), imperatives (for instructions), and present simple (to describe everyday events). Vocabulary: project, environment, classroom, plan, garden, trees, plants, meeting, participate, finish, plant, grow, water, clean, care, prepared, excited, ready.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-blanks):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences related to an environmental project. Students complete the sentences with the correct form of the verb (present simple, future simple, or an imperative) or a vocabulary word from a given box.
                        <br>Example:
                        <br>1. Tomorrow, we ______ (plant) new trees in the school field.
                        <br>2. _______ (Bring) your gardening gloves to the event.
                        <br>3. We usually ______ (collect) plastic bottles every Monday.
                        <br>4. Our ______ (project/plan) is to clean the park.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Scramble):</b> In pairs, students receive strips of paper with jumbled words to form grammatically correct sentences about environmental actions or plans. They must use the target grammar and vocabulary. They write down the correct sentences in their notebooks.
                        <br>Example: will / the / clean / We / environment / tomorrow. (Answer: We will clean the environment tomorrow.)
                        <br>Example: paper / the / in / bin / Put / blue. (Answer: Put the paper in the blue bin.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Writing & Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair is given an environmental topic (e.g., "Recycling at Home," "Cleaning the School Garden"). They collaboratively write 3-4 sentences describing everyday actions, future plans, or instructions related to the topic. After writing, pairs exchange their sentences with another pair for a peer check, focusing on correct grammar, spelling, and vocabulary use. They give suggestions for improvement using phrases like "Maybe you can add..." or "This sentence is great!".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15-17 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the acquired language creatively and fluently in a realistic communicative task. The focus is on conveying meaning and completing the task, encouraging collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will collaboratively write a short script (3-4 characters, 2-3 lines each) for a play about "An Environmental Project" happening at school or in their community. The script must include:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one sentence using the future simple (e.g., 'We will plant trees tomorrow.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one imperative for instructions (e.g., 'Bring a shovel to the park.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one sentence using the present simple to describe an everyday event (e.g., 'We wash the plastic and glass bottles every day.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least three vocabulary words from the list (e.g., project, environment, plant, clean, care, excited, ready).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples of pragmatic functions such as giving instructions/suggestions ('Let's recycle paper.') and describing everyday events ('We usually sort the bottles by color and size.').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher divides the class into small groups (3-4 students). Each group discusses ideas for their environmental project play (Sociolinguistic: discussing environmental actions). They appoint one student to write down the script collaboratively, with all group members contributing ideas and sentences. The teacher circulates, providing support and reminding students to incorporate the target grammar and vocabulary. The focus is on the written production of the script.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Scenario: A group plans a 'Garden Cleanup Day' at school.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative written product (short play script).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The script will revolve around "An Environmental Project" as part of "How Can We Help the Environment?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students' learning is assessed formatively, and they reflect on their progress. This stage provides feedback and encourages self-awareness of language development.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during collaborative writing and a simple rubric for the written script.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their written short script.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric categories for the written script):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammar (future simple, imperatives, present simple)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the script</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of collaborative effort (e.g., balanced contributions, ideas from all members visible in the script)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Completeness of the task requirements (e.g., number of lines, characters)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small piece of paper before leaving the classroom:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or grammar did you use when writing your script?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write the script with your group? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage allows for a review of the lesson's effectiveness by both students and teacher, informing future lesson planning and addressing student needs.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (written):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the script did I do well?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing I want to improve in my writing next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The collaborative writing activity worked well to engage students in writing and applying the grammar and vocabulary. Most students were able to correctly use imperatives and present simple. Some groups struggled with consistent use of future simple for plans, often mixing it with 'going to'. The peer checking activity helped students identify minor errors in spelling and sentence structure. I need to scaffold the future simple more explicitly in the next lesson to reinforce plans vs. intentions. I also observed that some students were more engaged in writing than others within their groups. Next time, I will assign specific roles for each group member during the writing phase to ensure more equitable participation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This written script will serve as the basis for the performance aspect in Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), where students will practice and present their plays, further solidifying their understanding and use of environmental vocabulary and structures in a communicative context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(2-3 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>